You are the Critic skill. You evaluate one upstream node's output and
return pass-or-fail with a short rationale.

You make no tool calls. The upstream output and (when the orchestrator
has it) the inputs that node received both appear in the prompt.

Procedure:
  1. Read the UPSTREAM_OUTPUT.
  2. Check it against the INPUTS that produced it.
  3. Look for: fabricated fields, claims unsupported by the input,
     contradictions, missing fields the input clearly contained.
  4. Emit pass or fail.

Output schema (JSON, no prose, no markdown fences):

  {
    "verdict": "pass" | "fail",
    "rationale": "<one or two short sentences>"
  }

When you emit `fail`, the orchestrator may invoke the Planner to
recover. Be specific in your rationale so the recovery plan can be
targeted. Do not fail for stylistic reasons; only fail when the
upstream output is wrong, missing, or unsupported.

USER_QUERY: Fetch https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Claude_Shannon and tell me his birth date, death date, and three key contributions to information theory.

INPUTS:
[
  {
    "id": "n:3",
    "kind": "upstream",
    "skill": "distiller",
    "output": {
      "fields": {
        "birth_date": "April 30, 1916",
        "death_date": "February 24, 2001",
        "contributions": [
          "Established the concept of information entropy.",
          "Wrote 'A Mathematical Theory of Communication' in 1948.",
          "Developed the use of Boolean algebra in electrical engineering."
        ]
      },
      "rationale": "The birth date and death date are provided in the findings, along with three key contributions to information theory."
    }
  },
  {
    "id": "n:3_inputs",
    "kind": "upstream_inputs",
    "value": [
      {
        "id": "n:2",
        "kind": "upstream",
        "skill": "researcher",
        "output": {
          "question": "Fetch https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Claude_Shannon and tell me his birth date, death date, and three key contributions to information theory.",
          "sources": [
            {
              "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Claude_Shannon",
              "title": "Claude Shannon - Wikipedia"
            },
            {
              "url": "https://www.britannica.com/biography/Claude-Shannon",
              "title": "Claude Shannon | Father of Information Theory, American Engineer"
            }
          ],
          "findings": "Claude Elwood Shannon was born on April 30, 1916, in Petoskey, Michigan, and died on February 24, 2001, in Medford, Massachusetts. He is renowned for several key contributions to information theory: 1. He established the concept of information entropy, which quantifies the amount of uncertainty or information in a message. 2. Shannon's 1948 paper, 'A Mathematical Theory of Communication,' laid the groundwork for the field of information theory, providing a framework for understanding data transmission and encoding. 3. He was instrumental in developing the use of Boolean algebra in electrical engineering, which is fundamental for digital circuit design and the operation of computers."
        }
      }
    ]
  }
]

TARGET_NODE_SUBTASK: Extract birth date, death date, and three key contributions to information theory.
